.TH zdock "" "Command"
.SH NAME
zdock \- desktop dock (icon bar)
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBzdock &\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.HS
zdock -- desktop dock (icon bar)
Usage:	zdock &
Started by the desktop rc script; one icon per catalog application.
Left click launches an app, or switches to its topmost window;
middle click starts another copy of a multi application.
.HE
.B zdock
is the dock of the
.B zview
desktop: an undecorated bar along the top of the screen showing one
icon per application of the dock's own catalog
.BR /usr/hr/etc/dock .
It is the desktop's
.I shell
in the old kernel-and-shell sense: the window server manages windows
and nothing else, and what the desktop offers \- the icons, launching,
switching \- lives in this ordinary, replaceable program, started from
the desktop start-up script
.B /usr/hr/etc/rc
like any other resident application.
The desktop survives without it; the server's right-click menu remains
as the fallback launcher.
The menu reads a separate catalog
.RB ( /usr/hr/etc/apps ,
see
.BR zview ),
so the dock's icons and the menu's entries are independent lists.
.PP
Each
.B dock
line reads
\fIname\fB:\fIpath\fB:\fImulti\fB:\fIicon\fB:\fIX\fB,\fIY\fB:\fIargs\fR
\- icon label (which must equal the application's declared title),
program, whether many copies may run (1) or one (0), the icon file
under
.BR /usr/hr/icons ,
where a launched window goes, and extra command-line arguments
(blank-separated) passed to the program at launch; all fields past the
program are optional, and
.B #
starts a comment.
A line starting with
.B @
declares a widget instead \- see WIDGETS below.
.PP
Every icon is always visible.
Its look tells the application's state:
.IP \fBup\fR
the picture sits high in the bar with the application's name beneath
\- nothing is running.
.IP \fBpressed\fR
the picture dropped well down in the bar, no name \- like a held-down
key: the application has at least one window (possibly hidden), or a
launch is in flight.
.PP
Clicks follow one convention:
.IP "\fBleft click\fR"
launches the application when none is running, and otherwise switches
to it \- the server raises the application's topmost window, restoring
a hidden one if need be.
.IP "\fBmiddle click\fR"
on a multi-instance application (multi = 1 in the catalog) starts
.I another
copy.
On a single-instance application it acts like a left click.
.PP
A launched window opens at the X,Y the catalog gives the entry (passed
to the application as
.BR "\-P X,Y" ),
or wherever the server places it when the entry names none.
While a launch is in flight the icon is already pressed and further
clicks are ignored, so a double click starts one copy, not two.
.SH WIDGETS
A catalog line
\fB@\fIname\fB:\fIpath\fR[\fB:\fIclick\fR][\fB:\fIargs\fR]
declares a
.IR widget :
a small resident program the dock starts itself, drawing live content
\- the time of day, free memory \- in an icon-sized cell at the
.I right
end of the bar (the first
.B @
line takes the rightmost cell; at most four).
The widget draws
.I inside
the dock's own window, clipped exactly as the dock is, so it can never
paint outside its cell; it has no icon and does not appear in the
window list.
The dock restarts a widget that dies \- a few times, then gives up and
leaves the cell blank \- and takes its widgets with it when it exits.
.PP
The
.I click
field makes the cell a button.
\fITitle\fB=\fIpath\fR
gives a left click on the cell the dock-icon verb for that
application: switch to it when it runs, launch it when it does not \-
so the application's own icon can be taken off the bar (the shipped
catalog wires the time cell to
.B zclock
and the memory cell to
.BR zmon ).
A click value of
.B *
opens the server's
.B "Switch to..."
dialog (the shipped program-count cell), the same dialog the desktop
menu offers.
.PP
The shipped widgets are
.BR zwclock ,
.B zwmem
and
.BR zwwin ;
they are started with a private
.B \-W
contract on the command line and are not meant to be run by hand.
.SH FILES
.DS
.ta 8n 30n
	/usr/hr/etc/dock	dock catalog
	/usr/hr/icons/*.icn	icon artwork; icon0.icn is the fallback
.DE
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.B zview
.SH NOTES
The bar is an ordinary window: other windows may cover it, and a left
click on it raises it again.
It cannot be hidden, moved or resized, takes no keyboard focus, and
does not appear in the desktop menu's
.B "Switch to..."
list.
.PP
The dock matches windows to catalog entries by window title: an
application's declared title must equal its catalog name (the
convention throughout the shipped catalog), or its icon will not read
as running.
The state of the bar follows the desktop immediately: the server
notifies the dock the moment the window list changes.
(A launch that dies before it makes a window is noticed by a slower
fallback poll, so its icon may stay pressed for a couple of seconds.)
